          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            Given a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homomorphism"} homomorphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/schemes"} schemes
            %em Grothendieck duality
            if the (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+functor"}> derived
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direct+image"} direct image
            functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_\ast
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasicoherent+sheaves"} quasicoherent sheaves
            has a (derived)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+adjoint"} right adjoint
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mo !
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^!
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Verdier+duality"} Verdier duality
            in a “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+context"}> Grothendieck context
            ” of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/six+operations"} six operations
          %p
            Grothendieck duality is intimately connected to
            = succeed "." do
              %strong dualizing complexes
            = succeed "." do
              %em Residues and Duality
          %h2#statement Statement
          %p
            Suppose
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\colon X \to Y
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-compact+map"} quasi-compact
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-separated+map"} quasi-separated
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"} morphism
            of
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/schemes"} schemes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/triangulated+functor"} triangulated functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi R
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %msub
                    %mi D
                    %mi qc
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{R}f_*\colon D_{qc}(X)\to D(Y)
            has a bounded below
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+adjoint"} right adjoint
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi R
                  %msub
                    %mi Hom
                    %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℱ
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mo ×
                  %mi 𝒢
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mo ∼
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi R
                  %msub
                    %mi Hom
                    %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi R
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                  %mi ℱ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi 𝒢
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{R}Hom_X(\mathcal{F}, f^\times \mathcal{G})\stackrel{\sim}{\to} \mathbf{R}Hom_Y(\mathbf{R}f_*\mathcal{F}, \mathcal{G})
            is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+isomorphism"} natural isomorphism
          %h2#dualizing_complexes Dualizing Complexes
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            be a noetherian scheme. A dualizing complex on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is a complex
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℛ
                  %mo ∈
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                    %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                    %mi Mod
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{R} \in \mathsf{D}(\mathsf{Mod} X)
            that has these three properties:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℛ
                      %mo ∈
                      %msubsup
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                          %mi D
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} c
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                        %mi Mod
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{R} \in \mathsf{D}^{\mathrm{b}}_{\mathrm{c}}(\mathsf{Mod} X)
                (i.e.
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℛ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{R}
                has bounded coherent cohomology sheaves).
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℛ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{R}
                has finite injective dimension.
            %li
              %p
                The canonical morphism
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝒪
                        %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} R
                      %msub
                        %mi ℋℴ𝓂
                        %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ℛ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi ℛ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}_X \to \mathrm{R} \mathcal{Hom}_{X}(\mathcal{R}, \mathcal{R})
                in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                        %mi D
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                        %mi Mod
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathsf{D}(\mathsf{Mod} X)
                is an isomorphism.
          %p
            The following two structures are basically equivalent to each other, for a given category of noetherian schemes
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                      %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathsf{S}
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                A psudofunctor
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                        %mo ↦
                        %msup
                          %mi f
                          %mo !
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \mapsto f^!
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong twisted inverse image
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi f
                        %mo !
                      %mo :
                      %msubsup
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                          %mi D
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} c
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                        %mi Mod
                      %mi Y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %msubsup
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                          %mi D
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} c
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                        %mi Mod
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      f^! : \mathsf{D}^{+}_{\mathrm{c}}(\mathsf{Mod} Y)
                      \\to \mathsf{D}^{+}_{\mathrm{c}}(\mathsf{Mod} X)
              %p
                to each map of schemes
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo :
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : X \to Y
                in
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                          %mi S
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathsf{S}
            %li
              %p
                A dualizing complex
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi ℛ
                        %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{R}_X
                for every scheme
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                in the category
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                          %mi S
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathsf{S}
          %p The relation between these two structures is demonstrated in the following Example.
          %p
            %strong> Example
            \. Suppose
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            is a regular finite dimensional noetherian ring, and let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                    %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathsf{S}
            be the category of finite type
            = succeed "-schemes." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo ↦
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mo !
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \mapsto f^!
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ∈
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                    %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \in \mathsf{S}
            with structural morphism
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mi X
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Spec
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_X : X \to \operatorname{Spec} K
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ℛ
                      %mi X
                    %mo :
                    %mo =
                    %msubsup
                      %mi π
                      %mi X
                      %mo !
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi K
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{R}_X := \pi_X^!(K)
          %p
            Conversely, suppose we are given a dualizing complex
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ℛ
                    %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{R}_X
            on each
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ∈
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                      %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \in \mathsf{S}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi D
                    %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D_X
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                        %mi D
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} c
                      %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                      %mi Mod
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathsf{D}^{}_{\mathrm{c}}(\mathsf{Mod} X)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                      %mi D
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} c
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                    %mi Mod
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathsf{D}^{+}_{\mathrm{c}}(\mathsf{Mod} X)
            with
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                        %mi D
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} c
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                      %mi Mod
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathsf{D}^{-}_{\mathrm{c}}(\mathsf{Mod} X)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi D
                    %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℳ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} R
                  %msub
                    %mi ℋℴ𝓂
                    %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℳ
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi ℛ
                    %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D_X(\mathcal{M}) := \mathrm{R} \mathcal{Hom}_{X}(\mathcal{M}, \mathcal{R}_X) .
          %p We then define
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mo !
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℳ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi D
                    %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} L
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi D
                    %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℳ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^!(\mathcal{M}) := D_Y( \mathrm{L} f^* ( D_X(\mathcal{M}) )) .
          %h3#rigid_dualizing_complexes Rigid Dualizing Complexes
          %p
            The notion of
            %em rigid dualizing complex
            was introduced by Van den Bergh in 1997, for a
            %em noncommutative ring
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            over a base field
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
          %p
            Yekutieli and Zhang have shown how to define a rigid dualizing complex
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi R
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R_{A/K}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            is a regular finite dimensional noetherian ring, and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is an essentially finite type
            = succeed "-ring" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            %em rigid residue complex
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒦
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{K}_{A/K}
          %p
            These rigid residue complexes have all the good functorial properties alluded to above, and even more. Specifically, they are covariant for essentially etale ring homomorphisms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi A
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \to A'
            (via the rigid localization homomorphism), and contravariant (as graded modules) for all ring homomorphisms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \to B
            (via the ind-rigid trace homomorphism).
          %p
            The rigid localization homomorphism permits the gluing of the rigid residue complexes
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒦
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{K}_{A/K}
            on affine open sets
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Spec
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U = \operatorname{Spec} A
            of a scheme
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            into a rigid residue complex
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒦
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{K}_{X/K}
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒦
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{K}_{X/K}
            on all essentially finite type
            = succeed "-schemes" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : X \to Y
            there is the ind-rigid trace homomoprhism
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Tr
                    %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒦
                    %mrow
                      %mi Y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒦
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi K
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Tr}_f : f_*(\mathcal{K}_{Y/K}) \to \mathcal{K}_{X/K} ,
          %p
            which is a homomorphism of graded quasi-coherent sheaves. The
            %em Residue Theorem
            says that when
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            is proper,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Tr
                    %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Tr}_f
            is a homomorphism of complexes. The
            %em Duality Theorem
            says that when
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            is proper,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Tr
                    %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Tr}_f
            induces global duality. As explained above, there is a corresponding functor
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mo !
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^!
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} R
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{R} f_*
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mo !
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^!
            are adjoint functors.
          %p
            Moreover, the rigidity method works also for finite type
            %strong Deligne-Mumford stacks
            over
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
